  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAfter a week of unsettled weather, a welcome mainly dry period\nof weather is expected through Saturday. A few isolated showers\nare possible across the Catskills this afternoon, but the large\nmajority of the area is expected to remain dry.\n\nThe high pressure system will move east of the area on Saturday,\nwhich will introduce a southerly return flow. This will result\nin warmer temperatures (highs in the 80s). \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe next deep trough and associated frontal boundary will slowly\nmove into the area on Sunday, particularly later in the day\nthrough Sunday night. PWATs will increase to around 1.8-1.9.\nAbundant moisture will advect into the region with southwesterly\nflow ahead of the front. This will result in widespread showers\nand thunderstorms, some which may result in heavy rainfall and\npotential flash flooding. The trend continues to be to slow the\nprogression of the trough, which therefore could delay the onset\nof rain to later in the day Sunday, or even Sunday evening. This\nalso would also result in showers and thunderstorms remaining in\nthe area on Monday. While severe thunderstorms cannot be\ncompletely ruled out on Sunday, this solution is appearing\nunlikely at this time due to the later timing and less\ninstability present.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA warm and humid airmass will remain in place Tuesday through\nthe end of the week. Temperatures will be mainly in the 80s\nduring this timeframe with dew points in the 60s to lower 70s.\nDaily instability-driven scattered showers and thunderstorms\nwill be possible, especially each afternoon. This will\nespecially be the case by the middle to end of the week as\nanother slow moving trough is expected to move into the area.",
